endobj
480 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 239 0 R
/E (Elevated comfort food, thoughtfully crafted)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
481 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 239 0 R
/E (That's how Matthew and Jessica describe their food, and they're quick to say what "elevated" doesn't mean. It doesn't mean complicated, fussy or pretentious. It means paying attention: good ingredients, real technique and care in every step.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
482 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 239 0 R
/E (You can see it in what they choose to cook with. The beef is Texas HeartBrand Akaushi. The bread comes from Kraftsmen Baking right here in Houston. The ice cream is Tillamook. And there's something you won't find in their kitchen at all: a conventional deep fryer. Take the Chicken Fried Steak, which is about as Texas comfort food as it gets. At Lynn's Table it starts with beef tenderloin, gets hand-breaded, then shallow-fried in beef tallow. Same dish you grew up on, made the way a classically trained chef would make it.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
483 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 239 0 R
/E (That's really Matthew's gift. Forty years of training doesn't show up as food you need a dictionary to order. It shows up as a burger or a chicken fried steak that's simply better than you expected. The same hands that turn out a beautifully prepared Beef Tournedos or Filet Oscar are the ones breading that steak, and the same care goes into both.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
484 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 239 0 R
/E (An Akaushi burger and a chef's special sit comfortably on the same menu, because to Matthew they deserve the same attention. Serious technique, without taking the fun out of comfort food.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
485 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 239 0 R
/E (If you only know Lynn's Table as a lunch spot or a sandwich stop during an afternoon of shopping, dinner is where all of this really comes alive. There's a weekly rhythm you can plan around: that tenderloin Chicken Fried Steak, slow-roasted Prime Rib, All-You-Can-Eat Spaghetti Bolognese and Lobster Friday.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
486 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 239 0 R
/E (And please, save room. Dessert is Jessica's side of the table: cakes, pies, bread pudding, plus sundaes, milkshakes, malts and floats made with that Tillamook ice cream. Her latest love is the house-made fudge, and with the holidays coming she's working on seasonal flavors and gift selections made for sharing.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
487 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 239 0 R
/E (Thirty years in, they're still cooking, still dreaming up new dishes and new flavors, and still looking ahead. The invitation hasn't changed: Come hungry, stay awhile and make yourself at home.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
